Dart SDK 3.13.3 (stable), captured 2026-09-19 in a scratch package (not hand-built), from a real
pub.dev resolution, using the pin dart.dev's security-advisories page uses as its own example:

    printf 'name: fix\nenvironment:\n  sdk: ">=3.0.0 <4.0.0"\ndependencies:\n  http: 0.13.0\n' > pubspec.yaml
    dart pub get                              # exited 0; printed "Dependencies are affected by
                                              # security advisories: [^0]: https://github.com/
                                              # advisories/GHSA-4rgh-jx4f-qfcq"
    dart pub outdated --json > pub_outdated.json   # exited 0

Top-level shape: a **dict** with one key, `packages`, a list. `pub outdated` lists only the
packages it has something to say about (three of the eleven resolved here: async, http, web),
each with `package`, `kind` (direct/dev/transitive), `isDiscontinued`, `replacedBy`,
`isCurrentRetracted`, `isCurrentAffectedByAdvisory` and four version slots `current`,
`upgradable`, `resolvable`, `latest`, each `{"version": ...}` or null. The advisory's id is
not in the JSON — only the boolean — which is why the parser's line points at `dart pub get`
for the URL. Only http is flagged; the two transitive entries have `current: null`, which the
parser has to tolerate.

The capture above is stdout only. `orc_test.runner.run` merges stderr into stdout, so any prose
a real `pub outdated` writes there would land ahead of this JSON — `audit_findings` reads from
the first `{` for exactly that reason; see test_audit_findings_skips_stderr_prefix in
test_lang_dart.py.
